<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting Our Country</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Interesting Things to See</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Mediate information from notices or announcements for broader understanding. Concept: Interpret graphical data for classroom use.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain basic information from local area notices or events, using gestures and visuals (e.g., "The museum is open on weekends," while pointing to weekend days on a calendar). Can interpret visitor information charts for peers and explain the significance of highlighted points (e.g., "This map shows the museum and beach locations. Visiting the museum first makes sense because it's closer to the bus stop."). Can summarize key details of an announcement by repeating main ideas (e.g., "The event starts at 3 p.m." while pointing to the time on a digital clock display).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of the lesson's focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a simple, authentic tourist map of a local area (e.g., Casco Viejo) with some key landmarks (e.g., "Plaza de la Independencia," "Metropolitan Cathedral," "French Plaza"). Students work in pairs to identify three "must-see" attractions. Then, the teacher asks them to imagine a new tourist arrived and could only understand basic Spanish. Students discuss how they would explain the location of these attractions and their significance in English, keeping it clear and simple for someone with limited comprehension. This activity encourages initial mediation of visual and cultural information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to mediate information from a simple notice. For example, the teacher shows a notice for a "Local Artisan Market" with details like "Open Saturdays & Sundays, 9 AM - 4 PM. Located next to the Old Town Square. Find unique handicrafts and local food!" The teacher then explains, "If a tourist asked me, 'When is the market open?', I would say, 'The market is open on weekends, from nine in the morning until four in the afternoon.' (pointing to the sign). If they asked, 'What can I find there?', I would explain, 'They said you can find unique handicrafts and local food there.' (using reported speech and visuals)." The teacher emphasizes clear articulation, gestures, and simplifying complex information for others, integrating vocabulary like 'location', 'market', 'unique', 'explain'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a friend doesn't understand this sign, what is my job?" (Expected answer: To explain it, simplify it, mediate the information.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I explain something to someone, should I use complicated words or simple words?" (Expected answer: Simple words, clear words.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting Our Country."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a "Panama City Tourist Information Board" image, featuring various graphical and textual elements:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A small map showing popular attractions (Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, Ancon Hill, Causeway) with icons.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Short notices for events: "Jazz Festival - Casco Viejo, Nov 15-17" and "BioMuseo Exhibit - Open daily, 10 AM - 4 PM. Admission: $12."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A section with 'Travel Tips': "Transportation: Buses & Taxis available. Safety: Always take precautions. Recommendations: Try local seafood!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A historical blurb: "Casco Viejo was founded in 1673. It has since become a UNESCO World Heritage site and a vibrant cultural area." (Integrating past simple and present perfect)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret graphical data for classroom use. Summarize key details of an announcement by repeating main ideas. Mediate information from notices or announcements for broader understanding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students answer questions based on the "Tourist Information Board" to practice information extraction:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the map, where is the BioMuseo located relative to the Causeway?" (Expected: near, in front of, next to)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What historical fact about Casco Viejo is mentioned? Has it changed over time?" (Expected: founded in 1673, has become a vibrant area)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: The Jazz Festival will be in the BioMuseo."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did the travel tips recommend for food?" (Expected: They said to try local seafood.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Mediate information using past simple and present perfect for contrasting actions over time, future with "will" and "going to," and reported speech. Vocabulary: nouns (attraction, plan, direction, market, museum, history, landmark, guide), verbs (explain, guide, describe, advise, arrange), adjectives (breathtaking, tranquil, unique, wonderful, famous, safe).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Announcement Interpretation (Controlled Practice).</b> The teacher provides students with short, distinct announcements (e.g., a bus schedule change, a museum special exhibit, a local restaurant's opening hours). Students complete cloze sentences, mediating the information. E.g., "The announcement ______ that the museum ______ a new exhibit, and it ______ open until 6 PM this week." (Expected: said, has launched/is showing, will be). Focus on reporting exact details using reported speech and future tenses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Chart to Explanation (Semi-controlled Substitution).</b> The teacher provides a simplified "Visitor Statistics Chart" for Panama (e.g., "Number of Tourists 2010 vs. 2023," "Most Visited Attractions"). In pairs, students formulate explanations based on the data, using past simple and present perfect. E.g., "In 2010, the Canal ______ (attract) X visitors, but since then, it ______ (attract) many more." "The report ______ (show) that Casco Viejo ______ (become) a famous landmark for tourists in the last five years." Students practice pronunciation of "interesting" and "restaurant" in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Directions Mediation (Pair Interaction).</b> Students receive cards: one with a simple map (e.g., from a bus stop to a "must-see" restaurant or a tranquil beach) and a starting point, the other with "visitor" questions (e.g., "How do I get to the seafood market from here?", "Is it safe to go alone?"). The "guide" student mediates directions and offers advice, using prepositions (go straight, turn right, across from, next to) and vocabulary (transportation, precaution, safety, recommend), while the "visitor" checks for understanding. They switch roles.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting Our Country."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Presentation – City Tour Brochure Mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students work in groups of 3-4. Their task is to present their "City Tour Brochure" project to the class (acting as potential tourists). Each group member must mediate different sections of the brochure, explaining the information clearly and concisely. They should:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explain the historical significance of landmarks using past simple and present perfect (e.g., "This park has been a favorite spot for families for years, and it was once the site of historic events.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Describe future plans or activities (e.g., "We will visit the Panama Canal in the morning, and we are going to try that new restaurant by the waterfront for lunch.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize key details from their brochure's text or graphical data (e.g., "The map shows the museum and beach locations. Visiting the museum first makes sense because it's closer to the bus stop.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Report recommendations or advice (e.g., "Our guide advised that the best seafood is found in the seafood market," or "They said that the view from Ancon Hill is breathtaking.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participate in group planning, offering directions and suggestions clearly, ensuring everyone understands, and practicing pronunciation of contrastive sounds (e.g., /st/ in "stay," /ʃ/ in "showcase").</li>
                    </ul>
                    Students use visuals from their brochures, gestures, and clear language to ensure the "tourists" understand the itinerary and attractions. The emphasis is on fluent, collaborative interaction and successful mediation of information.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy for task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "City Tour Brochure" to the class, mediating the information to their peers (acting as visitors).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion (Mediation):</b> Explains information from the brochure clearly, logically, and makes it easy to understand for the "visitors." (Learning Outcome: Can explain basic information... Can interpret visitor information charts... Can summarize key details...)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Accurately uses past simple and present perfect to describe history/changes, future with "will" and "going to" for plans, and reported speech for recommendations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Effectively integrates relevant vocabulary (e.g., location, attraction, landmark, guide, explore, recommend, breathtaking, unique) to describe the city tour.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation and Intelligibility:</b> Pronunciation is generally clear and intelligible, with attempts at contrastive sounds and reduced syllables (e.g., "interesting," "restaurant").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Works effectively with group members, ensuring shared responsibility and smooth transitions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one piece of information from our brochure that I think I mediated very well for my classmates?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing I could do better next time to explain information more clearly to a visitor?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and preparation for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did mediating information for the City Tour Brochure help me understand our city's attractions better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In what real-life situations outside of class might I need to mediate information for others?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in the mediation activities and the brochure presentation? Were students able to clearly explain information using the target grammar and vocabulary?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks? Were any specific grammatical structures (e.g., reported speech, present perfect) particularly challenging for students to mediate accurately?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Visiting Our Country" theme. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, likely building on the communicative skills developed here, perhaps through more complex descriptions or problem-solving scenarios related to travel and culture. The ability to mediate information will be a foundational skill for future communicative tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>